      "claim": "All existing deception‑detection technologies—including polygraphs, functional MRI, voice‑stress analysis, and thermal imaging—have maximum reported accuracies of roughly 60‑75 % in controlled studies, far below the near‑perfect (>95 %) accuracy implied by the word “accurately.”",
      "evidence": "National Research Council (2003) and National Academy of Sciences (2003) reports; meta‑analysis by Lykken (2015) showing polygraph sensitivity ~70 % and specificity ~60 %; fMRI deception studies (Kozel et al., 2005) averaging ~59 % accuracy; voice‑stress analysis studies averaging ~55 % (National Institute of Justice).",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "No deception‑detection machine has been validated to achieve high‑accuracy (>95 %) performance in real‑world or forensic settings; courts routinely reject polygraph and newer methods as unreliable evidence.",
      "evidence": "Daubert/Frye rulings (e.g., United States v. Scheffer, 1998) citing high error rates; Federal admissibility guidelines; lack of peer‑reviewed replication of >95 % accuracy claims.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Therefore any statement that a machine can “accurately” tell if someone is lying is unsupported by current scientific and legal evidence.",
      "evidence": "Logical conjunction of steps 1‑3: machines measure indirect correlates, their best‑case accuracies are far below the required threshold, and no validated high‑accuracy system exists.",
